fied management  this is an example of creating a new  dataproc metastore  in this case you can see the creation  screen for just creating a metastore  you can see that we only require a few  simple options things like  name the metastore version where you  want to create that metastore  whether you want kerberos to be enabled  or not this makes getting started with  the hive meta store very very  easy essentially one click  likewise we want the hive metastore to  be very compatible  so if you have an existing hive  metastore we want it to be very easy for  you to bring your data to the dataproc  metastore  in this example we have the import  functionality  this allows you to take an existing hive  metastore and import it  into a dataproc metastore so  it should be easy to create a new  metastore and play around with the hive  metastore and its tooling if you're a  new user  or if you're already deeply embedded and  invested in the hive metastore ecosystem  it should be very easy to migrate to use  the dataprop  metastore throughout the design  and implementation of the dataproc  metastore we've wanted to  create a service which is flexible  integrated  and reliable in terms of flexibility  as you're using the dataproc metastore  if you want to for example scale your  metastore up  because you have more users or you have  added more tooling which uses the hive  metastore  we want it to be very easy for you to  manage that metastore  the resources who can control uh who has  access to the meta store how it works  things like that  we also want that metastore to be  integrated as i  mentioned previously the hive metastore  sits at the center of an  open data ecosystem so it's really  important that the metastore  works with a lot of things out of the  box  as an example we've worked with clebra  and kuble to make sure  that they work out of the box with the  dataproc metastore  there are two examples but they showcase  that we're very focused on making sure  that the  proc meta store is open and integrated  meta stores are also very difficult to  run because they are a critical path  in your ecosystem if the meta store goes  down  often many things break so it's  important to us  that the dataproc metastore is reliable  and from the ground up we've designed  the dataproc metastore so it's  highly reliable  there's another really interesting  feature which i want to showcase  up front today and that is the dataproc  hub  let's start with two personas on one  hand you have a data scientist  and on the other you have an i.t  administrator and say that both of these  people or personas work for the same  organization  they have different needs the data  scientist wants to create notebooks that  are building blocks they want to create  models they want to tinker and  experiment and explore with data  and often data scientists want to do  this in a way that's unimpeded  so they want to be able to create a  notebook and use tooling  and often data scientists are  preferential to  frameworks and tooling so they just want  to get started and hands-on with data  on the other you have i.t administrators  who are concerned about things like  making sure that  code is production ready and  productionized property  they also care about costs and controls  and security  things that the data scientists might  not have  front and center in mind when they want  to get started with  a notebook what this usually means is  that when a data scientist wants to  create a notebook create a model and  then get something in production  it can take weeks if not months and  require a lot of steps  in between we think that  things should be much much easier the  data scientists should be able to  create a notebook and then take that  notebook  and have it turn into production ready  code or productionalize their notebook  as easily as possible  in essence notebooks should be easy  configurable  and all in one place there shouldn't be  a lot of steps  there shouldn't be a lot of friction and  there shouldn't be a lot of  organizational frustration and trying to  both enable notebooks and also control  them at the same time  so we built dataproc hub we recently  announced dataproc hub in open beta  and dataproc hub is built in conjunction  with the ai platform notebooks  functionality on google cloud platform  ultimately what this means is that we  are bringing data scientists and i.t  administrators together  and we're also bringing notebooks  together with ai  platform tools and dataproc as a result  data scientists can easily create  notebooks  they can choose their favorite open  source toolkits and frameworks  and they can use advanced features on  google cloud platform on dataproc  clusters things like gpus  or ssds if they have workloads that are  iops performant  what this means for it administrators is  that they can predefine  who can do what with notebooks what the  size and shape  of data clusters looks like what  features are automatically enabled or  disallowed  it also makes it very easy for these it  administrators to see exactly who's  using notebooks  and then take what's being done and  productionalize it very very  easily the it administrator story is  really important to us as we think about  notebook use and as the first  it's an optional but as a first step an  i.t administrator can pre-define  who can do what with dataproc up  specifically what this means is  an it administrator can predefine what  size  shape location and features are used in  a dataproc cluster that powers  a dataproc hub notebook that way once  somebody gets their jupiter-based  dataproc hub notebook  all of the constraints are automatically  applied for the cluster that runs and  powers that notebook  in this example we have a yaml-based  cluster configuration  that specifies an idle configuration so  in this case the cluster would be  deleted if it was idle for more than  7200 seconds  an id administrator can use this  configuration  as a template for clusters that are then  used for dataproc hub notebooks  so if a cluster is idle it automatically  gets deleted  and this showcases how we're trying to  bring both data scientists  and i.t administrators together with  dataproc up  ultimately the flow of using a notebook  with dataproc hub and ai platform  notebook  should be fairly easy the data scientist  can go look for examples or snippets in  a  ai notebooks platform or go directly to  dataproc hub  to create new notebooks or reuse  existing notebooks  then they can use that notebook and  dataproc or  bigquery to prepare process  create models data sets all the while  the it administrator  can see who's doing what with notebooks  they can pre-configure  the boundaries on what notebooks can do  and the clusters that  underlie them and then they can do  things like schedule and manage  notebooks that have already been created  by dataproc hub  ultimately this is showcasing that it  can be a frictionless process  between data scientists and i.t  administrators it also makes it very  easy to  create data proc clusters with  jupiter-based dataproc hub notebooks for  data scientists so there's  fewer hoops to jump through when you  want to do data science  so in this case i'm going to show you  what a quick demonstration of dataproc  hub  looks like to show you why it's really  easy to get started  with dataproc hub to do data science  with open source tools  so in this example i am a data scientist  and i want to maybe use spark and  bigquery  so i know i can go to ai hub and find  some examples for  common situations in this case i'm going  to see if there's an example for how to  use spark  with bigquery together so i search  for bigquery here and i will get a list  of results  and in the results i can see that there  is indeed a quick example showing how to  use spark with bigquery  this is using the spark bigquery  connector that's provided on dataproc  i then can take that example and open it  in the ai platform notebooks and i  already have a pre-configured workbench  that an it administrator has  pre-configured for me  i then sign in so dataproc hub does  perform the authorization and  authentication checks  and you can see that the cluster that is  behind this notebook has been already  pre-configured by the it administrator  so as a data scientist all i need to do  is just click start  this creates the notebook it also  creates the cluster that powers the  notebook behind the scenes  you can see in this example that all the  data scientists had to do  was click start and they got the  notebook and the cluster  they don't have to think about  configuration they don't have to think  about networking plumbing or cluster  options  the data scientist doesn't even  necessarily have to know what dataproc  is  all they did was create a notebook and  get started  beyond dataproc hub i want to talk  through some of the really  important open source updates that we've  provided on dataproc  in the last several months we make it a  point to keep up with the latest  and greatest in open source and we're  really passionate about  bringing new open source developments to  you in dataproc  as a managed service one of the big  advancements that i want to showcase  is dataproc 2 which just entered public  preview  dataproc 2 includes some significant  upgrades in the default components that  we install  on dataproc clusters what this means is  you get  hadoop3 spark3 hive 31  java 11 and several other upgrades to  components that we put on clusters by  default  we keep the number of components that we  install by default  somewhat slimmed down so you have  efficient clusters and we narrow the  installs down to the most used  components  but there's still a whole set of things  you can install  on dataproc clusters through  initialization actions  custom images optional components and  other means  these optional installs have also been  updated in dataproc image 2.  so it includes things like apache flink  updates to apache hbase  druid presto and others so dataproc  2 is a brand new image line in dataproc  designed to bring the hadoop 3 and  spark3 ecosystem  to you i specifically want to talk about  spark 3 because spark 3 includes a  number of  really interesting and important updates  that we're  excited both to bring you in dataproc  and we're very interested in  seeing how we can bring these  functionalities and improvements to  future dataproc features  so the theme for spark3 was performance  and there's three good examples of what  that means  for you in terms of performance in spark  3.  first spark 3 includes better adaptive  query support  so spark can optimize a query plan while  things are executing  spark 3 also has dynamic partition  printing so  unnecessary scans are avoided in queries  that  could look like data warehouse queries  so again faster querying  spark 3 also has improved gpu  support and we're really excited because  we think there's a future where spark 3  could run  entirely on gpus both for data science  and potentially just general data  processing so  we're very interested in seeing what the  future holds for spark and gpus  there's also a lot of advancements for  spark running on  kubernetes i'll talk about it in just a  bit  but dataproc has a flavor which allows  you to run spark on kubernetes clusters  that are managed by dataproc  so we're very excited to see new  advancements in kubernetes and spark  because it will  only make both the spark community  better  but also makes dataproc a much more  interesting product from a kubernetes  perspective  i mentioned gpus before but this is  something that's that's really really  exciting to us  and we've worked closely with nvidia to  make sure that  gpus work well in spark and also that  gpus work well on gcp and dataproc  clusters  so you can create dataproc clusters with  nvidia gpus today  we've also worked with nvidia on things  like the rapids frameworks to make sure  that you can do really high performance  data processing and data science on  dataproc clusters  if you're interested in using spark3  with gpus  i would highly encourage you to look at  the documentation on the dataproc site  and also the rapids framework  we think this is a really interesting  avenue for spark and open source  in the long term to run on gpus  it's very easy to get started with  dataproc 2 today  all you need to do is specify that you  want to run the preview  of dataproc so this is an example of a  cloud sdk command or gcloud  that specifies the preview image i also  want to call out how easy it is to use  different sets of open source components  on dataproc clusters  you don't have to think about upgrading  one whole cluster to new versions of  spark or hadoop  if you want to try out a new version of  spark in this case spark3  create a very small new cluster and use  the preview image  try a job out try some of the new  features out then you can delete the  cluster when you're done  dataproc imaging is a subtle feature but  makes it very  very easy and interesting to try out  different sets and versions of open  source components  without fear of breaking clusters that  are running production or  high value jobs there's also a whole set  of new dataproc features that i want to  run you through as well  as i mentioned we have a flavor of  dataproc which runs  on kubernetes we recently announced  dataproc  on kubernetes engine clusters in beta  what this means is that you can run  spark jobs with dataproc  on gke clusters we are really excited  about the future  of running open source on kubernetes  clusters  i'll give you three examples of why one  we think  that it allows better resource  management so you can have multiple  things running on one cluster and be  a little bit more sure that resources  are being balanced and managed properly  it also allows you to take one  kubernetes cluster and run open source  data processing with something like a  web application  if you were so inclined it also  potentially allows for better job  isolation  and as customers get better resource  management  job isolation is something that goes  hand in hand  we're also really interested in both  seeing  and helping these open source frameworks  and toolkits work with some of the more  advanced and interesting features on  kubernetes things like auto scaling node  healing  and the like because that allows for  more dynamic clusters over time  the dataproc team has been involved in  getting both spark and flink working  on kubernetes last year we released both  an operator for spark  and kubernetes because it's a common  question i do want to call out that we  will support both yarn  and kubernetes well into the future of  dataproc we're not  making a wholesale decision to switch  from one to the other  we see customers over the next several  years will want to use both  for different reasons and we have a plan  to support both yarn and kubernetes well  into the future another feature that i'm  really  excited to mention is dataproc flex mode  i'll explain flex mode through a problem  with both scaling  and virtual machines when you're trying  to scale down you may hold on to  a node or a set of nodes because they  have intermediate data they've finished  processing  they're just holding on to something  that they can't get rid of but otherwise  you would get rid of the node  likewise with preemptables you may have  a node that's finished  processing or reached a state where it  can checkpoint  but the node is preempted in both cases  if you removed the node you would lose  the data  flex mode is a stateful data store that  allows you to store  stateful data such as mapreduce shuffle  data in hdfs  on the cluster this makes clusters more  flexible because you can add  and remove clusters much more easily  without interrupting your job  so in my example if you wanted to scale  down once the node's done  computing it can put all of its data in  flexpool  and then the node can simply be auto  scaled down  likewise if you lose a preemptable vm  that's finished processing  that preemptable vm has already put its  data in the flex pool so its preemption  presents no risk to your job flex mode  should allow better auto scaling  and also should allow you to use more  preemptables in your dataproc clusters  which again means greater flexibility  and cost optimization  there's two new security features which  adding to the  pile of pre-existing data proc security  features  so dataproc already allows you to use  security features such as  ranger customer managed and customer  supplied encryption keys  kerberos vpc service controls  our component gateway which allows you  to access web uis with no networking  it still provides authorization all of  these are great but customers have told  us they want  more security which we've been squarely  focused on and these are two of multiple  new security features which you'll see  in the future  one of the security features is secure  extensions for notebooks  what this means is that you can give  somebody a notebook  and that someone or that data scientist  for example  their credentials will be used for  execution of the job  and to communicate with whatever  services that job  connects with so for example if i am a  data scientist  and i interact with cloud storage my  data scientists individual credentials  be used to go talk to cloud storage  instead of a service account and this  brings better per  user isolation and security to notebooks  a second feature that we recently  announced was shielded vms  so if you're using dataproc clusters  with uh ubu218 or debian 10  you can use shielded vms again  these are two new out of future  security improvements that you'll  continue to see in dataproc to  make sure that customers have all of the  security features both that they  might want but also that we could  potentially offer as a cloud provider  i want to finally mention uh something  that doesn't get a lot of attention but  is a  kind of a continual feature in dataproc  and that's additional compute  flexibility  every time there's a new region or a new  vm type  or new accelerator accelerator we try to  wire those through to dataproc as  quickly as possible  what this means is you get new levers  for cost  and speed optimization in this example  say that you had a workload which was  performance insensitive and you just  wanted to lower your cost  you could use e2 machine types to lower  your cost relative to maybe you are  running on  n1 standard forms you can see in the  table all of the vms kind of look and  feel the same but the cost is different  this is an example of how additional  compute flexibility can give you some  really powerful levers to  speed things up or to optimize costs  depending on  your needs finally i want to talk about  how you can get started with  dataproc the best place to get started  with dataproc  is our documentation on the google cloud  site we also have a set  of tutorials on the dataproc site things  like using spark with bigquery or  creating clusters to run  presto so you should be able to run  through those tutorials to understand  dataproc and some of the open source  tooling pretty quickly  if you're brand new to google cloud i  really encourage you to take a look at  the  free trial for google cloud platform to  get started with both google cloud  and also databrock if you're curious  about dataproc you're an existing  dataproc  customer and want to know more or  participate  we have a stackoverflow tag google cloud  dataproc  which you can use to find questions and  ask new questions  often you'll see the dataproc  engineering team trying to help  customers and stack overflow users  we also have a google group cloud  dataproc discuss  and a feedback email list and please do  send us your feedback and thoughts it's  customers that  largely influence and help drive our  roadmap so  we are passionate on hearing and  responding to our customers and whatever  needs  you may have with that i want to thank  you very much for your time  and have a great day

